  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nWednesday-Friday:\nSlightly cooler temperatures behind the frontal passage on\nWednesday, and lingering through Friday. Temperatures will range\nfrom middle/upper 80s (east) to lower 90s (west). While\nwidespread extreme heat is not in the forecast, it is plausible\nsome locations push heat index values around 100 in southeast KS\ninto far southwest MO through the end of the week. However,\nconfidence is not high enough to issue any additional heat\nheadlines at this time. Otherwise, expect a few systems to\ntranslate through the pattern mid to late week, with scattered\nshower and thunderstorm chances (30-60%) returning Wednesday\nnight into Thursday morning. The coverage looks to remain\nconfined to a narrower corridor, with many locations remaining\ndry. Dry time will be particularly prevalent through the\nafternoon and evening on Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nBy Thursday night, a stronger shortwave trough ejects out of the\nNorthern Plains. Moisture return looks healthy (PWATs 1.8-2.0\ninches) ahead of this next system and associated frontal \npassage. Ensemble guidance trends suggest this system tracks a \nbit further east than previous forecasts. There is still some \nspread across the models and members, and thus lingering \nuncertainty still exists on the exact timing and location of the\nhighest rain chances on Thursday night through Friday. For now,\nthe highest chances (40-70%) are focused across the north and \neastern portions of the area. A strong thunderstorm wind gust or\ntwo is possible within any bands/clusters, though confidence \nremains low. An additional hazard of focus is the potential for \nlocalized heavy rainfall. Anywhere that realizes repeated \nthunderstorms over the same locations in a short period of time \nmay be subject to flash flooding. This activity lingers across \nthe area through Friday night, before gradually shifting out of \nthe area on Saturday. \n\nSaturday-Monday:\nMuch cooler on Saturday behind the frontal passage, with\nunseasonably cool temperatures in the forecast. The latest NBM\nguidance depicts highs ranging from upper 70s to lower/middle\n80s. Lows dip into the lower to middle 60s. This prevails into \nSunday as well, with an upper level low meandering over the \nregion. Low isolated shower chances (10-20%) exist this weekend,\nthough coverage would be limited. Temperatures look to \ngradually warm back up into early next week with a building \nridge to the west.",
